lesson k-first-strokes v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=2 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "First strokes on the lines"
  goal "Start a stroke at the top, pull it down or round to the line, and tell a tall letter from a small one."

  look {
    scene 30x8 {
      handwriting rules show="l" rows=1 trace=3 width=26 at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      These are handwriting lines. Letters sit on the bottom line. A small letter reaches the dotted
      line, and a tall letter like l goes all the way to the top. Every stroke starts at the top:
      put the pencil on the top line and pull it straight down.
      """
  }
  do {
    show k.trace-down x="l"
    level easy {
      worked k.first-move x="l" level=medium
    }
    practice k.first-move count=2 seed=2
    show k.trace-round x="c"
    practice k.tall-or-small count=3 seed=3 easy-count=2
    show k.my-name
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.tail-below k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.count-strokes x="o" easy-x="l" hard-x="t"
  }
  remember {
    say "Start at the top. Sit the letter on the line."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The tracing and the name are for you to look at, not the machine: the answer sheet says what
    to look for. Watch where the pencil starts rather than how neat the letter is, since the start
    is what makes writing quick later. A pencil held near the point between the thumb and first
    finger, resting on the middle finger, is what to aim for; chunky crayons help small hands. If
    the child is stuck, draw big strokes in the air or in sand first. Off the page, draw rain
    falling down and snails curling round on a big sheet of paper. England, Reception, ELG Fine
    Motor Skills: "Hold a pencil effectively in preparation for fluent writing".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level l is looked at in the teacher's pen first, the letters to judge are l, o, h and a, and the three-star's letter is l, one stroke."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the letters to judge include t, which is only a little taller than a small letter, the tails are p and j, and the three-star's t takes two strokes."
  }
}
